      "Language": "fre",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "Pancreatic metastases from renal carcinoma are poorly known. The aim of this study was to report clinical and radiological manifestations, the treatment performed, and the observed survival in 7 patients with this rare entity."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "All patients were operated on. One patient had nonresectable tumor. Six patients underwent curative resection. There was one postoperative death. Follow-up after pancreatectomy ranged from 6 months to 3 years. Two patients developed extra-pancreatic metastases one year and 3 years after pancreatectomy respectively."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Pancreatic metastases from renal carcinoma are rare and often occur several years after nephrectomy. However their resection is often possible and allows a good long-term survival."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS AND METHODS",
          "content": "From 1988 to 2000, 7 patients (5 men and 2 women, mean age = 66 years) with pancreatic metastases from a renal cell carcinoma were observed in the same center. One patient had synchronous metastasis; in the 6 others, metastases were diagnosed 4 to 16 years after nephrectomy, and were revealed by pain (n = 2), gastrointestinal bleeding (n = 1), faintness (n = 1) or routine follow-up (n = 2). The diagnosis of metastases was made by contrast-enhanced abdominal CT-scan."
        }
      ]},
